# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Anglia
# Ford Anglia 100E: the 2-door volume Anglia of 1953-1959. Launched in October
# 1953 as Ford of Britain's three-box replacement for the upright E494A Anglia;
# 345,841 built to October 1959, when the 105E replaced it and the 100E body
# continued as the Popular 100E to 1962. The 4-door twin of the same body (Ford
# Prefect 100E, 100,554 built to 1959) is in the tree as FordPrefectLate; this is
# the cheaper 2-door Anglia.
#
# The E494A Anglia (1949-53) is not modelled separately: it shares its body
# family and front-end styling with the E93A/E493A Prefect already modelled as
# FordPrefectEarly (1938-1953), the only material difference is the number of
# doors, which the pak-scale model does not show, and no source reached gives
# Ford of Britain paint names for 1948-53. Precedent: the Morris Cowley is
# represented by the two Morris Oxford models.
#
# Dimensions (the sources disagree by more than the model can show at pak
# scale): Anglia 100E 3,854 x 1,537 x 1,454 mm, wheelbase 2,210 mm (Wikipedia
# "Ford Anglia", 100E infobox; width and height from The Motor, 1954); Prefect
# 100E 3,861 x 1,448 x 1,486 mm (The Motor, 1957); Popular 100E 3,804 x 1,543 x
# 1,492 mm (The Motor, 1960).
#
# Colours: Black, Corfe Grey, Sarum Blue, Wells Fawn and Hereford Green are real
# Ford of Britain paint names, taken from the 100E Anglia de Luxe colour list in
# the April 1957 UK brochure (Ford publication C5543/457, scan on archive.org):
# "Anglia de Luxe colours available - Ivory, Sarum Blue, Hereford Green, Corfe
# Grey, Warwick Green, Wells Fawn, Carlile Blue, Black", with the standard 100E
# listed in Wells Fawn, Corfe Grey and Black only. The 1953-56 launch-range paint
# names were not found in any source reached, so the evidenced April 1957 range
# is used across the model; the earlier palette may have differed in name. Ivory,
# Warwick Green and Carlile Blue are omitted as the less common of the eight.
#
# Colour shares: the DVSA first-registration table starts with 1994
# registrations, so its measured base for cars first registered in 1994-96 (blue
# 23-25%, red 19-23%, white 7-10%, black 8-10%, grey 4-5%, silver 9-11%, other
# 20-25%) is the starting point, adjusted for mid-1950s registrations: silver
# (9-11% measured) was not a mass-market colour before the 1970s metallics and is
# omitted; black and grey are weighted up (both were near their post-war peak in
# the 1950s and both were standard 100E colours); green and fawn/beige come out
# of the measured "other" group, which was far larger before the 1970s; cream/
# ivory (7-10% measured) is the next colour and is omitted. Shares used: blue
# 24%, black 23%, grey 19%, fawn 17%, green 15%.
#
# DistributionWeight totals 320 across the five colourways, provisionally: the
# model is a strong non-anchor tiered below the 1955/1960 anchor (Morris Minor,
# 500 on the 2026-11-15 rebase) at roughly two-thirds of it, from the production
# ratio (345,841 100E Anglias against about 885,000 Minors built by end-1960),
# adjusted for the Minor's larger export share and longer accumulation of stock.
# Provisional pending the central rebase. The 100E and 105E overlap briefly in
# road presence (1959-64), so their combined weight exceeds the anchor's 500 in
# that window; this is a property of the per-generation convention and is for the
# central rebase to resolve.
#
# Speed 115 km/h: a game-balance figure, not the model's top speed. The Motor
# recorded 70.2 mph (113 km/h) for a 100E saloon in 1954. Comfort 45/398 sits at
# the contemporary small-car tier (Morris Minor 45/398, FordPrefectLate 45/398).
#
# Dates describe when each colour would be seen regularly on UK roads, not
# production dates. Black, Corfe Grey and Wells Fawn enter at the October 1953
# launch as the three documented standard-car colours; the Deluxe-only colours
# are staggered on observed road presence (estimates). Production ended in
# October 1959 but the 100E body ran as the Popular 100E to 1962, hence the road
# tails to 1964-67. The modelled front end is the 1957-59 mesh-grille form with
# chrome bumpers. The photo-referenced body has a separate sloping rear window
# and a rounded boot with a shallow crown, rather than a hatchback rear.
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/ford-anglia-100e-photo.blend in the blends repository.
# Geometry and final colour renders approved on 2026-11-15.

